course: biology
lesson: 111
title: Feeding times and the zoo kitchen
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Behind every zoo is a busy kitchen where keepers prepare hundreds of meals a day, and each one is planned for one kind of animal, or even one single animal, by a zoo nutritionist who works rather like a doctor.
explain: The food is chosen to match how each animal eats in the wild: whole carcasses for big cats and vultures, frozen and thawed mice for snakes, live crickets for frogs and lizards, leafy greens for monkeys and a mushy ant-like mix for the anteater.
explain: Feeding is also a job for the brain: gorillas find their food scattered in the grass, apes get big pieces to hold and bite, and big cats even have days with no food at all, just like their wild cousins.
explain: That is why the sign says «please do not throw food to the animals»: every meal is measured, and a crisp from each of a thousand visitors adds up to a sick, fat animal.
fact: Zoo kitchens prepare hundreds of meals a day, and nutritionists plan the right mix of food for each kind of animal.
fact: Food is cut to suit each mouth: tiny pieces for small beaks and big pieces for apes, so they hold, bite and work as in the wild.
fact: Many zoos give big cats one or two fast days a week, because wild cats do not eat every day and fast days stop zoo cats getting fat.
fact: Big cats are often fed whole carcasses with bones and fur, which are good for their teeth, and vultures are fed whole dead animals too.
fact: Snakes and birds of prey are fed mostly frozen, thawed mice, rats and chicks, which is safer for the snake because a live rat can bite it; keepers often warm the food, because snakes find it by smell and warmth.
fact: Many zoos breed their own crickets and mealworms, and dust them with vitamin and calcium powder before feeding them to frogs and lizards, because insects alone do not have enough of what those animals need.
fact: Feeding times are when many animals are most lively, so zoos hold public feeding talks, and some change the times daily so animals do not just wait at the gate.
fact: Scattering food in the grass makes gorillas walk and search, which is called foraging, and keeps them busy for longer.
fact: Anteaters get a specially made mushy mix that copies an ant diet, because a zoo cannot collect enough wild ants.
fact: Farm fruit is bred to be much sweeter than wild fruit, so many zoos give it only as a treat; some British zoos stopped giving monkeys bananas and gave leafy vegetables instead.
fact: Visitors' food can poison animals, make them fat or carry germs, and animals have choked on plastic bags and coins thrown by visitors.
exercise: Who plans the diet of each animal in a big zoo? | A nutritionist, rather like a doctor for food | The visitors who bring snacks | The animals choose from a menu | The zoo shop manager
exercise: Why are vegetables cut tiny for small birds but left in big pieces for apes? | Food is cut to suit each mouth and how the animal eats | Apes are not allowed small food by law | Small pieces are cheaper to buy | It is just for looks
exercise: Why do many zoos give big cats a fast day with no food? | Wild cats do not eat every day, and it stops them getting fat | The keepers take a day off from cooking | Big cats only need food once a month | Hunger makes big cats friendlier
exercise: Why are big cats often fed whole carcasses with bones? | Chewing bones and fur is good for their teeth | Lions can only digest bones | Zoos throw away all the meat first | It makes the cats sleep all day
exercise: What do zoos mostly feed their snakes? | Frozen mice and rats, thawed before feeding | Fresh fruit and vegetables | Bread soaked in milk | Live rabbits every day
exercise: Why do keepers often warm a thawed mouse before giving it to a snake? | Snakes find their food by smell and warmth | Snakes cannot swallow anything cold | Warm mice taste of chocolate | Snakes have no sense of smell at all, only good eyes
exercise: Why are crickets dusted with powder before a lizard eats them? | Insects alone lack some vitamins and calcium reptiles need | The powder makes the crickets sleepy | The powder hides the crickets | Lizards will only eat white food
exercise: Why do some zoos change feeding times every day? | So animals do not just wait | Because the keepers forget the time | Because animals cannot tell the time anyway | So that visitors never see a feeding
exercise: What is foraging? | Searching around for food, as gorillas do in the forest | Sleeping after a big meal | Fighting another animal of your own kind for its food and water | Drinking water from a river
exercise: Why does the anteater eat a mushy mix from a bowl? | A zoo cannot collect enough wild ants, so the mix copies an ant diet | Anteaters actually hate ants | Anteaters have lost all their teeth from eating sweets given by visitors | It is the cheapest food in the kitchen
exercise: Why do many zoos give sweet farm fruit only as a treat? | It is much sweeter than wild fruit | Fruit is poisonous to every zoo animal, even in very small amounts | Fruit is too expensive for zoos to buy | Animals cannot taste sweet food at all
exercise: How are vultures often fed at a zoo? | With a whole dead animal | With seeds poured into a hanging feeder like garden birds | With fish thrown into a pond | With live insects in a box
exercise: Why does the sign say «please do not throw food to the animals»? | Visitors' food can poison or fatten animals and carry germs | The animals are never hungry | The zoo wants to sell its own snacks | Animals cannot eat anything people eat, not even plain bread
exercise: Penguin feeding draws a crowd. Why do zoos hold public feeding times? | Animals are lively then, and keepers can teach visitors about them | It is the only time in the whole day that the animals ever wake up | The penguins demand an audience | Feeding must be watched by law
exercise: «zoo's got 3 penguins at the front and 2 gorillas behind, how many legs is that» | 10, two for each penguin and two for each gorilla | 20, four for each animal | 6, one for each penguin and one for each gorilla | 12, two for each penguin and three for each gorilla
test: «my mate says lions in zoos get a massive steak every single day» What is right? | Many zoos give big cats fast days, because wild cats do not eat daily | Yes, because a lion that misses even one meal will soon fall ill and die | Zoo lions only eat vegetables now | Lions are fed once a year in winter
test: Peppa-style picture books show monkeys munching bananas all day. What do many zoo monkeys get? | Mostly leafy vegetables, with sweet fruit only as a treat | Only bananas, three times a day, as that is all monkeys eat | Only meat and fish | Only crisps given by visitors
test: What would happen if every one of a thousand visitors threw a monkey one crisp? | The monkeys would get far too much and could get fat or ill | Nothing, one crisp is too small to matter | The monkeys would refuse every crisp | The keepers would stop feeding them their meals, so it evens out
test: «why don't they just chuck a live rat in with the python, it's natural innit» What is the best answer? | A live rat can bite and hurt the snake, so thawed food is safer | Pythons are scared of the dark | Live rats run far too fast, so the python would never catch one at all | Pythons only eat plants in zoos
test: The snake ignores a cold mouse from the freezer. What should the keeper try? | Warming it, because snakes find food by smell and warmth | Painting it a brighter colour | Shouting so the snake wakes up | Giving the snake a fresh carrot instead, as snakes like vegetables
test: «bloody hell, they just dump the gorillas' food in the grass, lazy keepers» What is really going on? | Scattering makes gorillas forage, which keeps them busy like in the wild | The keepers dropped the tray by accident | Gorillas can only digest food that has touched the soil, for its minerals | It is a test of whether gorillas can count
test: A pangolin needs a diet very like the anteater's. Why is it so hard to keep in zoos? | Its special ant and termite diet is very hard to copy | Pangolins eat a whole cow a day | Pangolins only eat zoo visitors' lunches | Pangolins need fresh snow every day
test: Why might a whole melon be better than a chopped one for an elephant? | It makes the elephant work at it, like food in the wild | Chopped melon is poisonous to elephants | Elephants cannot see small pieces of fruit at all with their tiny eyes | A whole melon has more water inside it
test: In Madagascar the zoo animals are fed from a neat tray every morning. What would a real zoo kitchen do for a lion? | Give whole carcasses some days and no food on fast days | Serve it a steak with a knife and fork | Feed it salad like the zebra | Give it a fresh tray of fish every hour, like the penguins get
test: «mealworms in boxes behind the scenes? gross, why» What is the reason? | Many zoos breed their own insects to feed frogs, lizards and birds | They are pets for the keepers | Mealworms clean the zoo floors | They are grown in boxes to be sold as crunchy sweets in the zoo shop
test: «how many tails at the vulture feeding, there's 4 vultures and, oh wait, 5» | 5, one tail for each vulture | 4, as first said | 10, two tails each | 0, birds do not have tails
exam: Identify the principal reason for which many zoological institutions withhold food from large felids on one or two days per week. | Wild felids do not feed daily, and such fasting prevents obesity | Keeping staff are not employed on those days | Felids are incapable of digesting food upon any two consecutive days | Hunger renders the animals more docile towards visitors
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the feeding of serpents in zoological collections. | They are customarily given frozen rodents, thawed and frequently warmed | They are given live prey of the largest size available, daily and without exception | They are sustained exclusively upon vegetable matter | They are fed solely by members of the visiting public
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of dusting insects with a powder prior to feeding? | It supplies vitamins and calcium in which insects alone are deficient | It renders the insects invisible to the predator | It sedates the insects for the convenience of the keeper and the reptile | It imparts a sweet flavour preferred by reptiles
exam: Determine the reason for which visitors are prohibited from casting food to animals. | Unmeasured food may poison or fatten animals and transmit pathogens | The animals are already provided with an excess of food | The institution wishes solely to protect the sale of its own refreshments | Animals are by nature incapable of consuming human food
